WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
 So now we just finished talking about basic confidentiality using simple Caesar cipher.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.000
 And we'll get better before we'll be more sophisticated than that because Caesar ciphers are trivially breakable, obviously.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:15.000
 But now we're going to switch from confidentiality to integrity.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:23.000
 And we're worried about the message, just to review, that confidentiality means we're hiding information.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:28.000
 We just don't want Eve to see it because Eve sees the ciphertext.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.000
 And we want her to never be able to extract the plain text.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:37.000
 And as we saw in the last lecture, you, if I'm just using a Caesar cipher,

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.000
 I mean, there was little or nothing I could do to stop you from doing it.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.000
 All you'd do is enough work and you would figure out the shift and then you'd have everything.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.000
 So it would take you, you know, you'd write a program.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.000
 It would take you like a thousandth of a second to check all possible things and you're done.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:57.000
 So that's confidentiality. Now we're going to talk about integrity, right?

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:02.000
 And we're going to kind of assume confidentiality or perhaps assume it's not necessary

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.000
 because we're transporting it in a locked box or in a whatever.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:11.000
 But let's just say I had some piece of paper, right?

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:16.000
 And you'd want it to know if this piece of paper really came from you.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.000
 Well, we would use things like a signature, right?

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:28.000
 Or we would use a bit of wax where we would push the, our seal on it and the seal only belonged to us.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.000
 But, but really did signatures can be forged.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:35.000
 You know, people can steal the wax imprint thing.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:39.000
 I mean, in Roman times they would wear it around their neck to make sure that no one stole it.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:43.000
 But you could also just create a fake one so you could seal your letter with the wax.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:50.000
 But, and you break the wax seal. But in the computer world, we need something, right?

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:57.000
 We need a situation where, where perhaps, I mean, my wife had to get a prescription

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.000
 and they sent her an email with a prescription in it.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.000
 And this prescription had on the end of it a digital signature.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:10.000
 It was just a bunch of numbers. And you think to yourself, wow, you know, here's this prescription.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.000
 And at the end it just has a bunch of numbers and that's the digital signature from the doctor.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:19.000
 And how does that work? Well, it, it works surprisingly well.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:23.000
 And, and that message can be forwarded. She can print it out.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:28.000
 She can scan it. She can send it to a pharmacist via email.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:34.000
 And as long as that signature's in there, we can know that the data originally came from the doctor.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:38.000
 And that, that you didn't, no one modified it to be a different kind of prescription.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:41.000
 Like a different amount or a different drug.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:45.000
 And, and then that would invalidate the signature. And how exactly is that done?

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:50.000
 Well, it's done using a technique called cryptographic hashing.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:57.000
 And it is a bit of computer software, a bit of code that takes a large amount of text

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:06.000
 and reduces it down to some small set of numbers, a large block of data to a fixed length set of numbers.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:12.000
 And, and the message is the big thing and the digest is the little thing.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:16.000
 It's sort of, it's like it digests it and sort of gives you this little tiny thing.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:24.000
 Now the key is there are many different techniques to, to map from a message to the hash or the digest.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.000
 And some are better than others.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:32.000
 And it turns out that there is a long-term whole field of mathematics and computer science

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:37.000
 that is dedicated to understanding what a good cryptographic hash might be.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:43.000
 And so there, there are these well-known cryptographic hashes that you may have heard of, like SHA-1 or MD5.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:51.000
 Each of those is the result of many, many years of research of thinking through what a good cryptographic hash is.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:58.000
 So, for example, one cryptographic hash might be, I sign it with a number of characters in the message.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:03.000
 That would be something that says, like, well, at least they didn't expand or contract the number of characters in the message.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:08.000
 But then at some point that would be such an obvious thing that you would change the signature as well.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:14.000
 So you want to make it so you can't change the message and change the signature because then you're sort of properly forging a signature.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:18.000
 So the hash function that takes the message to create the digest,

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:25.000
 that's something that is a scientific mathematical research effort to get the thing right.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:31.000
 Okay. So here is an example of a hash function.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:36.000
 Now, this hash function takes as message input on one side, right?

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:41.000
 It takes message input of, and this can be short, medium, or extremely long messages.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:46.000
 And the digest is always a fixed size. It's always a fixed size, right?

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:53.000
 Some get longer than others. Different message hashing functions give different length, but they're all fixed.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:59.000
 And they're fixed even if the input is megabytes. It can be megabytes, hundreds of megabytes.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:04.000
 And you can still run all that through the hash function and get a digest.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:14.000
 And so the key thing is to make it so that for any change in the input, the digest also changes.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:21.000
 And so here we have the red fox jumps over the blue dog, right? And that's the hash that comes out.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:27.000
 And if we change one letter, you know, the V to a U, the hash changes dramatically.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:32.000
 So this hash changes dramatically. So this suggests this is a good cryptographic hash function, right?

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:40.000
 The length didn't change. All the characters are the same, but one character changed and the hash function changes completely, okay?

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:52.000
 And here is another flipping of characters, right, where from here to here, excuse me, better color here. Which color is that?

00:05:52.000 --> 00:06:00.000
 From here to here, the V and the E were just toggled. And yet from here to here, the hash function is completely different.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:06.000
 And so the hash function needs to generate quite different hashes, even with tiny perturbations of the input.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:10.000
 You're not even allowed to change one character or add or remove character.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:19.000
 And they know when a cryptographic hash function is bad, if they can take two different messages and send it through and get the same digest.

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:23.000
 And so there's a lot of research to try to, as soon as they come up with one of these things,

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:28.000
 there is a massive amount of research to try to disprove it, to say that's a bad one.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:33.000
 And the bad one is if two different messages come in and they come out with the same hash function,

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:44.000
 then that's bad because it means that it's provable, provably that the signature could use, the same signature could be used to sign two different input messages.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:50.000
 Okay, so this hash function, function is a bit of computer code, right?

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:59.000
 And, you know, SHA1, you can go look on Wikipedia for SHA1 or MD5. These are kind of classic hash functions.

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:07.000
 And when you read the SHA1 or MD5 Wikipedia, they'll talk about the fact that it's been decided it's kind of flawed.

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:10.000
 And so there's like SHA1, don't use it. It's not cool.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:14.000
 Now you can use it for less critical things. You just have to be aware of what its limitations are.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:18.000
 So like SHA256 is better than SHA1.

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:24.000
 So what happens is there's continuous research and there's continuous improvement in the mathematics of these hashing functions.

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:31.000
 And they're getting really good at it because we've been using these things for a very, very, very long time.

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:34.000
 Now, if you want to play, let me clear this up.

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:42.000
 So I've got a simple SHA1 calculator and you'll be using this in the homework.

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:52.000
 The SHA1 calculator takes as input some kind of a plain text and it produces output when you hit the thing.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:08:00.000
 So you can put in Pony or Fluffy or whatever, or even a whole bunch of stuff, and it produces a SHA1.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:08.000
 So basically, get ready to use this because the next upcoming exercises are going to use this.

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:17.000
 And you can read SHA1 about SHA1 on Wikipedia and you can find out like, yeah, it's seen as less than perfect and you should use SHA256 or whatever.

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:29.000
 But actually, lots of applications in less than critical situations know that for short and reasonably length strings, the flaws are mathematically found, but they're not commonly run into.

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:34.000
 So SHA1 is not horrible. It's just been proven sort of less than ideal.

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:46.000
 And so for highly sensitive information, you would never use SHA1, but for simple things, SHA1 and even things like MD5 are commonly used, like for hashing passwords.

